-- title:
--   Nilpotence of coordinates passes to composites
-- statement:
--   Let $B$ be a commutative ring and let $\varphi, \psi$ be elements of `Series B`, that is, pairs $(\varphi_0,\varphi_1)$, $(\psi_0,\psi_1)$ of formal power series in two variables over $B$ (functions $\mathrm{Fin}\,2 \to B[[x_0,x_1]]$). Assume that each component of $\varphi$ has vanishing constant coefficient, so that substitution of $\varphi$ into a power series is defined. Assume further that there is an $N \in \mathbb{N}$ with $x_i^{N}$ in the ideal of $B[[x_0,x_1]]$ generated by the two components of $\varphi$ for $i = 0,1$, and that there is an $M \in \mathbb{N}$ with $x_i^{M}$ in the ideal generated by the two components of $\psi$ for $i = 0,1$. The conclusion is that there exists $K \in \mathbb{N}$ such that for $i = 0,1$ the power $x_i^{K}$ lies in the ideal generated by the range of `Series.comp`, i.e. by the two series $\psi_j(\varphi_0,\varphi_1)$, $j = 0,1$, obtained by substituting $\varphi$ into the components of $\psi$. No hypothesis is imposed on the constant coefficients of $\psi$, and none on $B$ beyond commutativity.
--
--   The statement records that the condition "some power of each coordinate lies in the ideal generated by the components" — the nilpotence of the coordinates modulo that ideal, the algebraic expression of a pair of series defining a finite-type quotient at the origin — is stable under composition of pairs of series. It is used in the study of formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-modules in the Cerednik–Drinfeld part of the development, where it feeds the verification of this property for the series giving the action of $\mathbb{Z}_p$-points via structure constants.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_CerednikDrinfeld_FormalODModule_exists_X_pow_mem_span_comp_of_X_pow_mem_span.lean

